  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that isorhamnetin-3-O-acetylglucoside may interact with ME-180 through a series of molecular targets. Specifically, the compound is linked to the EZH2/SUZ12/EED complex, which is a key component of the polycomb repressive complex 2 (PRC2). This complex is known to regulate gene expression by modifying histone tails, and it is a target of several small molecules, including Ponfolin and Broussoaurone A. These molecules, in turn, are targeted by isorhamnetin-3-O-acetylglucoside, indicating a potential mechanism by which the compound may modulate gene expression through the PRC2 complex. The evidence is consistent across multiple paths, with similar scores, suggesting a robust relationship. However, the exact mechanism by which isorhamnetin-3-O-acetylglucoside influences ME-180 remains to be fully elucidated, and further experimental validation is needed to confirm the biological relevance of these interactions.",
